From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 22:23:11 +0200
Subject: leds: lgm-sso: invert the duty cycle for active-low pins

An active-low LED on this controller cannot be turned off. Both of the
states the driver could reach hold its pin low, so the lamp is lit in
either one.

SSO_CPU bit N selects what drives pin N: 0 parks the pin low, 1 hands it
to DUTY_CYCLE(N). Writing the requested brightness to the duty register
raw and then driving the bit through gpiod_set_value() gives, for an
active-low pin, brightness 255 -> bit 0, pin parked low, lit; and
brightness 0 -> bit 1 with duty 0, engine holding the pin low, lit
again. Dark needs bit 1 with duty 0xFF, which no input can produce.

Invert the duty for an active-low pin and derive the enable bit from the
value actually written, then write that bit raw so that gpiolib does not
invert it a second time. This is the shape of the vendor driver this one
was rewritten from; the inversion is what the rewrite dropped.

Deriving the bit from the written duty rather than from the requested
brightness is load-bearing for dimming, not just tidiness: bit 0 parks
the pin and defeats the engine entirely, so a dimmed active-low LED has
to keep bit 1 and vary the duty.

sso_led_hw_cfg() needs the same treatment, and additionally has to stop
making the enable write conditional on a non-zero brightness. An
active-low LED whose default-state is off needs the bit set so that the
engine holds its pin high; left alone, it comes up lit. For an
active-high LED the write is either a no-op or parks a stray pin low,
which is that LED's off state, so nothing regresses.

Behaviour on active-high pins is otherwise unchanged: without the
inversion the duty is the brightness, gpiod_set_raw_value() and
gpiod_set_value() agree, and the enable bit is the old value.

With this change an active-low LED declared default-state = "off" is
dark from the moment the driver probes, and dimming keeps the engine
driving the pin.

Hardware blink is unaffected, and has limits of its own. At full
brightness an active-low pin has a duty of 0, which parks the pin and
leaves the engine nothing to drive, so such an LED blinks under the LED
core's software timer instead. And sso_led_hw_cfg() enables the engine
for every hw_blink LED at probe regardless of the brightness requested,
which a later patch addresses. No in-tree device tree sets
intel,sso-hw-blink.

Fixes: c3987cd2bca3 ("leds: lgm: Add LED controller driver for LGM SoC")
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/drivers/leds/blink/leds-lgm-sso.c b/drivers/leds/blink/leds-lgm-sso.c
index b546875b491d..68e367cb4888 100644
--- a/drivers/leds/blink/leds-lgm-sso.c
+++ b/drivers/leds/blink/leds-lgm-sso.c
@@ -243,6 +243,29 @@ static void sso_led_freq_set(struct sso_led_priv *priv, u32 pin, int freq_idx)
 	regmap_update_bits(priv->mmap, reg, GENMASK(high, low), val);
 }
 
+/*
+ * SSO_CPU bit N selects what drives pin N: 0 parks the pin low, 1 hands it to
+ * DUTY_CYCLE(N). Both states this driver could reach therefore light an
+ * active-low LED -- bit 0 parks the pin low, and bit 1 with a raw duty of 0
+ * has the engine hold it low -- so an active-low LED could never be darkened
+ * at all. Dark is bit 1 with duty 0xFF.
+ *
+ * Invert the duty for an active-low pin, as the vendor driver this one
+ * descends from does, and derive the enable bit from the value actually
+ * written rather than from the requested brightness. Intermediate brightness
+ * depends on that pairing: bit 0 parks the pin and defeats the engine, so a
+ * dimmed active-low LED has to keep bit 1 and vary the duty. The bit is then
+ * written raw, because the inversion has already been applied here and
+ * gpiod_set_value() would apply it a second time.
+ */
+static u8 sso_led_duty(struct sso_led *led, enum led_brightness brightness)
+{
+	if (led->gpiod && gpiod_is_active_low(led->gpiod))
+		return LED_FULL - brightness;
+
+	return brightness;
+}
+
 static void sso_led_brightness_set(struct led_classdev *led_cdev,
 				   enum led_brightness brightness)
 {
@@ -250,13 +273,15 @@ static void sso_led_brightness_set(struct led_classdev *led_cdev,
 	struct sso_led_desc *desc;
 	struct sso_led *led;
 	int val;
+	u8 duty;
 
 	led = cdev_to_sso_led_data(led_cdev);
 	priv = led->priv;
 	desc = &led->desc;
 
 	desc->brightness = brightness;
-	regmap_write(priv->mmap, DUTY_CYCLE(desc->pin), brightness);
+	duty = sso_led_duty(led, brightness);
+	regmap_write(priv->mmap, DUTY_CYCLE(desc->pin), duty);
 
 	if (brightness == LED_OFF)
 		val = 0;
@@ -274,7 +299,7 @@ static void sso_led_brightness_set(struct led_classdev *led_cdev,
 	}
 
 	if (!desc->hw_trig)
-		gpiod_set_value(led->gpiod, val);
+		gpiod_set_raw_value(led->gpiod, duty != LED_OFF);
 }
 
 static enum led_brightness sso_led_brightness_get(struct led_classdev *led_cdev)
@@ -339,6 +364,7 @@ sso_led_blink_set(struct led_classdev *led_cdev, unsigned long *delay_on,
 static void sso_led_hw_cfg(struct sso_led_priv *priv, struct sso_led *led)
 {
 	struct sso_led_desc *desc = &led->desc;
+	u8 duty;
 
 	/* set freq */
 	if (desc->hw_blink) {
@@ -352,11 +378,17 @@ static void sso_led_hw_cfg(struct sso_led_priv *priv, struct sso_led *led)
 				   1 << desc->pin);
 
 	/* set brightness */
-	regmap_write(priv->mmap, DUTY_CYCLE(desc->pin), desc->brightness);
-
-	/* enable output */
-	if (!desc->hw_trig && desc->brightness)
-		gpiod_set_value(led->gpiod, 1);
+	duty = sso_led_duty(led, desc->brightness);
+	regmap_write(priv->mmap, DUTY_CYCLE(desc->pin), duty);
+
+	/*
+	 * Enable output. Unconditionally, not only for a non-zero brightness:
+	 * an active-low LED that starts out off needs the enable bit set, so
+	 * that the engine holds its pin high rather than the pin being parked
+	 * low and the lamp coming up lit.
+	 */
+	if (!desc->hw_trig)
+		gpiod_set_raw_value(led->gpiod, duty != LED_OFF);
 }
 
 static int sso_create_led(struct sso_led_priv *priv, struct sso_led *led,
